1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,941
This is ECE 358, video 5 b. The topic is HTTP response time and HTTP messages. Video 5 a explained non-persistent and persistent HTTP. Now we use it to solve problems.

2
00:00:18,391 --> 00:00:25,389
After this video, you can write the response time for the five scenarios of table 1 in the question bank.

3
00:00:25,839 --> 00:00:29,679
You can decide if parallel or persistent connections help.

4
00:00:30,129 --> 00:00:35,121
You can read every line of an HTTP request and response message.

5
00:00:35,571 --> 00:00:38,963
And you can use the four methods and the status codes.

6
00:00:39,413 --> 00:00:52,021
Question 21. A client downloads a web page that is one file with no references. Derive the response time with RTT and F T T. Pause the video and try it.

7
00:00:52,471 --> 00:00:59,660
Use the time diagram of slide 22. The first RTT opens the TCP connection.

8
00:01:00,110 --> 00:01:06,915
The second RTT carries the request to the server, and the first bits of the response back to the client.

9
00:01:07,365 --> 00:01:15,835
Then the rest of the file arrives in one F T T. The F T T is the file size divided by the transmission rate.

10
00:01:16,285 --> 00:01:20,829
State the assumptions. The small messages have no transmission time.

11
00:01:21,279 --> 00:01:25,673
And the close is not counted, because the file has already arrived.

12
00:01:26,123 --> 00:01:35,467
Add the three parts: RTT, plus RTT, plus F T T. The response time is 2 RTT plus F T T.

13
00:01:35,917 --> 00:01:46,051
Question 25 is the main calculation question of this part. A page has one file and N referenced objects. All of them are F bits.

14
00:01:46,501 --> 00:01:49,146
The server rate is T x bits per second.

15
00:01:49,596 --> 00:01:52,625
The RTT is the same for every server.

16
00:01:53,075 --> 00:01:56,830
Closing a TCP connection takes one RTT.

17
00:01:57,280 --> 00:02:09,589
You must close a connection before you open a new one between the same end points. The end points are the source IP, the source port, the destination IP, and the destination port.

18
00:02:10,039 --> 00:02:15,885
All the required throughput is available. Pause the video and try all five scenarios.

19
00:02:16,335 --> 00:02:26,745
Table 1 gives scenario 1: non-persistent HTTP with N equal to 0. The answer is 2 RTT plus F over T x.

20
00:02:27,195 --> 00:02:32,678
The bar shows one object. One RTT opens the TCP connection.

21
00:02:33,128 --> 00:02:37,800
One RTT carries the request and the first bits of the response.

22
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:40,810
Then F over T x sends the file.

23
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:58,924
Scenario 2 is persistent HTTP with N equal to 0. There is only one object. Persistent HTTP still opens one connection and sends one request. So the answer is the same: 2 RTT plus F over T x.

24
00:02:59,374 --> 00:03:10,830
Scenario 3: non-persistent HTTP, N equal to 3 referenced objects on the same server, sequential connections. Pause the video and try it.

25
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:18,917
The base page needs one connection: an open RTT, a request RTT, and F over T x.

26
00:03:19,367 --> 00:03:26,791
Assume that the client uses the same source port each time. Then the next connection has the same end points.

27
00:03:27,241 --> 00:03:32,617
So the client must first close the old connection. That costs one RTT.

28
00:03:33,067 --> 00:03:38,017
Then object 1 needs its own open, request, and F over T x.

29
00:03:38,467 --> 00:03:44,269
The same happens for object 2 and object 3: a close, and then a new connection.

30
00:03:44,719 --> 00:03:55,685
Count the bars. There are 4 objects, and each object takes 2 RTT plus F over T x. That is 8 RTT plus 4 F over T x.

31
00:03:56,135 --> 00:03:59,356
There are 3 closes, so add 3 RTT.

32
00:03:59,806 --> 00:04:03,881
The total is 11 RTT plus 4 F over T x.

33
00:04:04,331 --> 00:04:12,288
In this drawing, F over T x is one RTT. So the bars end at 11 plus 4, which is 15.

34
00:04:12,738 --> 00:04:21,933
In general, with N objects: N plus 1, times 2 RTT plus F over T x, plus N RTT for the closes.

35
00:04:22,383 --> 00:04:31,471
Scenario 4: the 3 referenced objects are on 3 different servers. The connections are sequential. Pause the video and try it.

36
00:04:31,921 --> 00:04:39,366
Each new connection goes to a new server, so it has different end points. The client does not wait for a close.

37
00:04:39,816 --> 00:04:48,755
So the answer is 4 times 2 RTT plus F over T x. That is 8 RTT plus 4 F over T x.

38
00:04:49,205 --> 00:04:55,797
Scenario 5: the 3 objects are on the same server, and the connections run in parallel.

39
00:04:56,247 --> 00:05:01,900
The client must get the base file first. It parses the file to find the references.

40
00:05:02,350 --> 00:05:09,966
Then it opens 3 connections at the same time. Each connection has its own source port, so no close is needed.

41
00:05:10,416 --> 00:05:15,387
All the throughput is available, so each file still takes F over T x.

42
00:05:15,837 --> 00:05:24,093
The answer is 2 times 2 RTT plus F over T x. That is 4 RTT plus 2 F over T x.

43
00:05:24,543 --> 00:05:37,193
The table collects the five results. The general form of scenario 3 is N plus 1, times 2 RTT plus F over T x, plus N RTT for the closes.

44
00:05:37,643 --> 00:05:44,577
The general form of scenario 4 is N plus 1, times 2 RTT plus F over T x.

45
00:05:45,027 --> 00:05:53,411
Now a numeric check, with our own numbers: RTT is 40 milliseconds, and F over T x is 10 milliseconds.

46
00:05:53,861 --> 00:05:59,706
Scenarios 1 and 2 give 2 times 40, plus 10, which is 90 milliseconds.

47
00:06:00,156 --> 00:06:07,665
Scenario 3 gives 11 times 40, plus 4 times 10, which is 480 milliseconds.

48
00:06:08,115 --> 00:06:15,411
Scenario 4 gives 8 times 40, plus 4 times 10, which is 360 milliseconds.

49
00:06:15,861 --> 00:06:23,136
Scenario 5 gives 4 times 40, plus 2 times 10, which is 180 milliseconds.

50
00:06:23,586 --> 00:06:31,671
The bars compare the times. With referenced objects, the parallel connections of scenario 5 give the shortest time.

51
00:06:32,121 --> 00:06:41,380
Scenario 5 depends on an assumption. If one parallel connection reuses the port of the base connection, it must wait for the close.

52
00:06:41,830 --> 00:06:56,315
Then the answer is 5 RTT plus 2 F over T x. With our numbers, that is 5 times 40, plus 2 times 10, which is 220 milliseconds. In the exam, state your assumption.

53
00:06:56,765 --> 00:07:05,683
Problem set 2, problem 5. The link is 10 meters long, and very slow: 150 bits per second in each direction.

54
00:07:06,133 --> 00:07:09,183
A data packet is 100,000 bits.

55
00:07:09,633 --> 00:07:12,236
A control packet is 200 bits.

56
00:07:12,686 --> 00:07:17,081
N parallel connections each get 1 over N of the link rate.

57
00:07:17,531 --> 00:07:26,363
Each object is 100 kilobits. The base object references 10 objects on the same server. Pause the video and try it.

58
00:07:26,813 --> 00:07:36,050
First, one object on one connection. The client and the server exchange 3 control packets: two for the open, and the request.

59
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:45,780
Each control packet takes 200 over 150, which is 1.33 seconds. So 3 of them take 4 seconds.

60
00:07:46,230 --> 00:07:54,017
Then the object takes 100,000 over 150, which is 666.7 seconds.

61
00:07:54,467 --> 00:08:06,563
One object takes 4 plus 666.7, which is 670.7 seconds, plus 4 propagation delays, one for each packet.

62
00:08:07,013 --> 00:08:18,490
The propagation delay, T p, is 10 meters over 3 times 10 to the power 8 meters per second. That is about 0.033 microseconds.

63
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:23,228
So T p is negligible. The transmission time dominates.

64
00:08:23,678 --> 00:08:30,441
Now the parallel case. The base object takes 670.7 seconds, as before.

65
00:08:30,891 --> 00:08:38,891
Then 10 parallel connections share the link. Each gets 150 over 10, which is 15 bits per second.

66
00:08:39,341 --> 00:08:46,359
Each connection needs 3 control packets: 3 times 200 over 15, which is 40 seconds.

67
00:08:46,809 --> 00:08:54,553
Its object takes 100,000 over 15, which is 6666.7 seconds.

68
00:08:55,003 --> 00:09:13,073
The total is 670.7 plus 40 plus 6666.7, which is 7377 seconds. Add 8 propagation delays: 4 for the base object, and 4 for the parallel connections.

69
00:09:13,523 --> 00:09:25,107
Compare with non-persistent connections one after the other: 11 times 670.7 is also 7377 seconds.

70
00:09:25,557 --> 00:09:30,314
So parallel connections do not help, because they share the same link rate.

71
00:09:30,764 --> 00:09:45,804
Now persistent HTTP. After the base object, each object needs only a request and the data: 1.33 plus 666.7, which is 668 seconds.

72
00:09:46,254 --> 00:09:58,585
The total is 670.7 plus 10 times 668, which is 7351 seconds, plus 24 propagation delays.

73
00:09:59,035 --> 00:10:09,381
Persistent HTTP saves two control packets per object: 10 times 2 times 1.33, which is 26.7 seconds.

74
00:10:09,831 --> 00:10:19,517
26.7 of 7377 seconds is 0.36 percent, less than 1 percent.

75
00:10:19,967 --> 00:10:32,895
So the official answer: parallel downloads do not make sense, and persistent HTTP gives no significant gain. On this link, the transmission time of the data dominates.

76
00:10:33,345 --> 00:10:45,739
Problem 6 uses the same link. Bob shares it with 4 other users. Each connection gets an equal share of the 150 bits per second. Pause the video and try it.

77
00:10:46,189 --> 00:10:55,896
Part a. Bob opens 10 parallel connections. The other 4 users have one each. That is 10 plus 4, which is 14 connections.

78
00:10:56,346 --> 00:11:05,541
Bob gets 10 of 14 connections. That is 71.4 percent of the link, or 107.1 bits per second.

79
00:11:05,991 --> 00:11:11,111
So yes, parallel connections help Bob. He gets a larger share of the link.

80
00:11:11,561 --> 00:11:19,881
Part b. All five users open 5 parallel connections. That is 5 times 5, which is 25 connections.

81
00:11:20,331 --> 00:11:25,536
Each user has 5 of 25 connections, which is 30 bits per second.

82
00:11:25,986 --> 00:11:33,431
If Bob alone used only one connection, there are 1 plus 4 times 5, which is 21 connections.

83
00:11:33,881 --> 00:11:47,983
Then Bob gets 150 over 21, only 7.1 bits per second. Each other user gets 5 times 150 over 21, which is 35.7 bits per second.

84
00:11:48,433 --> 00:11:55,622
So yes, Bob must still use parallel downloads. Otherwise, he gets less bandwidth than the other users.

85
00:11:56,072 --> 00:12:00,723
HTTP has two types of messages: request and response.

86
00:12:01,173 --> 00:12:04,479
Both are ASCII text, so humans can read them.

87
00:12:04,929 --> 00:12:11,265
The figure shows the request message of slide 24. The first line is the request line.

88
00:12:11,715 --> 00:12:14,467
The request line starts with the method, GET.

89
00:12:14,917 --> 00:12:18,907
Then comes the object, slash index dot html.

90
00:12:19,357 --> 00:12:22,621
Then comes the version, HTTP 1.1.

91
00:12:23,071 --> 00:12:29,257
The next lines are header lines. Each header line has a field name, a colon, and a value.

92
00:12:29,707 --> 00:12:32,417
The Host line gives the name of the server.

93
00:12:32,867 --> 00:12:36,088
The User-Agent line gives the browser type.

94
00:12:36,538 --> 00:12:42,938
The Accept lines say what the browser can use: the file types, the languages, and the encodings.

95
00:12:43,388 --> 00:12:46,972
Connection: keep-alive asks for a persistent connection.

96
00:12:47,422 --> 00:12:53,715
Each line ends with a carriage return and a line feed, written backslash r, backslash n.

97
00:12:54,165 --> 00:12:59,904
A line with only a carriage return and a line feed marks the end of the header lines.

98
00:13:00,354 --> 00:13:09,399
Slide 25 gives the general format. The request line has three fields. The first field is the method, for example GET.

99
00:13:09,849 --> 00:13:15,332
The second field is the URL, for example slash index dot html.

100
00:13:15,782 --> 00:13:20,689
The third field is the version, for example HTTP 1.1.

101
00:13:21,139 --> 00:13:23,955
A space, s p, separates the fields.

102
00:13:24,405 --> 00:13:27,711
A carriage return and a line feed end the line.

103
00:13:28,161 --> 00:13:36,481
Then come the header lines. Each header line has a field name, a colon, a value, and a carriage return and line feed.

104
00:13:36,931 --> 00:13:42,435
A blank line, with only a carriage return and a line feed, ends the header lines.

105
00:13:42,885 --> 00:13:51,099
Last is the entity body. For a GET request, the body is usually empty. A POST request puts data in the body.

106
00:13:51,549 --> 00:13:58,482
Slide 26 gives four methods. The first is POST. A web page often includes form input.

107
00:13:58,932 --> 00:14:04,927
The user input goes from the client to the server in the entity body of the POST request.

108
00:14:05,377 --> 00:14:13,505
GET can also send a small amount of data to the server. The data goes in the URL field, after a question mark.

109
00:14:13,955 --> 00:14:17,645
In the example, the user data is monkeys and banana.

110
00:14:18,095 --> 00:14:25,797
HEAD asks for the headers only. These are the headers that the server would return to a GET request for the same URL.

111
00:14:26,247 --> 00:14:30,151
No object is sent: the response to HEAD has no body.

112
00:14:30,601 --> 00:14:32,990
PUT uploads a new file to the server.

113
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:39,115
PUT completely replaces the file at the URL with the content of the entity body.

114
00:14:39,565 --> 00:14:45,965
The figure shows the response message of slide 27. The first line is the status line.

115
00:14:46,415 --> 00:14:51,620
The status line starts with the protocol version, HTTP 1.1.

116
00:14:52,070 --> 00:14:54,609
Then comes the status code, 200.

117
00:14:55,059 --> 00:14:57,448
Then comes the status phrase, OK.

118
00:14:57,898 --> 00:15:02,783
Then come the header lines. Date is the time when the server sent the response.

119
00:15:03,233 --> 00:15:05,303
Server names the server software.

120
00:15:05,753 --> 00:15:09,678
Last-Modified is the time when the object last changed.

121
00:15:10,128 --> 00:15:16,848
Content-Length gives the size of the object in bytes: 2651 bytes.

122
00:15:17,298 --> 00:15:21,117
Content-Type says that the object is an HTML file.

123
00:15:21,567 --> 00:15:26,473
After the blank line comes the data: the requested HTML file.

124
00:15:26,923 --> 00:15:37,291
The ETag is a label that the server gives to an object. If the object changes, it gets a new label. This avoids redundant data transfer.

125
00:15:37,741 --> 00:15:48,429
The status code is in the first line of the response message. Code 200, OK, means that the request succeeded. The object is later in the message.

126
00:15:48,879 --> 00:15:56,559
301, moved permanently, means that the object moved. The new location is in the Location field.

127
00:15:57,009 --> 00:16:02,343
400, bad request, means that the server did not understand the request.

128
00:16:02,793 --> 00:16:08,019
404, not found, means that the document is not on this server.

129
00:16:08,469 --> 00:16:18,048
505, HTTP version not supported, means that the server does not support the HTTP version of the request.

130
00:16:18,498 --> 00:16:25,111
Problem set 2, problem 1. Five true or false statements. Pause the video and try them.

131
00:16:25,561 --> 00:16:32,217
Part a. The page has some text and 3 images. That is 1 plus 3, which is 4 objects.

132
00:16:32,667 --> 00:16:41,243
Each object needs its own request. So the client sends 4 requests and receives 4 responses. Part a is false.

133
00:16:41,693 --> 00:16:53,917
Part b is true. Both pages come from the same server, w w w dot m i t dot e d u. So one persistent connection can carry both pages.

134
00:16:54,367 --> 00:17:01,557
Part c is false. A non-persistent connection carries at most one object, so only one request.

135
00:17:02,007 --> 00:17:11,991
Part d is false. The Date header is the time when the server sent the response. The Last-Modified header gives the time when the object changed.

136
00:17:12,441 --> 00:17:21,294
Part e is false. For example, the response to a HEAD request has an empty body. These answers match the official solution.

137
00:17:21,744 --> 00:17:36,016
Problem 2 gives a GET message that Wireshark captured. Find the URL, the HTTP version, the connection type, the IP address of the client, and the browser type. Pause the video and try it.

138
00:17:36,466 --> 00:17:42,823
Part a. The Host field gives the server name: gaia dot c s dot umass dot e d u.

139
00:17:43,273 --> 00:17:50,228
The request line gives the file: slash c s 453 slash index dot html.

140
00:17:50,678 --> 00:18:01,622
Together they make the URL: gaia dot c s dot umass dot e d u, slash c s 453, slash index dot html.

141
00:18:02,072 --> 00:18:11,224
Part b. The version is in the request line, just before the first carriage return and line feed. It is HTTP 1.1.

142
00:18:11,674 --> 00:18:17,989
Part c. The Connection field says keep-alive. So the browser requests a persistent connection.

143
00:18:18,439 --> 00:18:29,788
Part d. The IP address of the client is not in the HTTP message. It is in the IP datagram that carried the TCP segment with this request.

144
00:18:30,238 --> 00:18:37,598
Part e. The User-Agent field gives the browser type: Mozilla 5.0, Netscape 7.2.

145
00:18:38,048 --> 00:18:45,536
The server needs the browser type, because it can send different versions of the same object to different types of browsers.

146
00:18:45,986 --> 00:18:51,213
Problem 3 gives the response to that GET message. Pause the video and try it.

147
00:18:51,663 --> 00:18:57,828
Part a. The status code 200 and the phrase OK show that the server found the document.

148
00:18:58,278 --> 00:19:08,219
The Date field gives the time of the reply: Tuesday, 7 March 2008, 12 39 and 45 seconds, G M T.

149
00:19:08,669 --> 00:19:17,885
Part b. The Last-Modified field: Saturday, 10 December 2005, 18 27 46, G M T.

150
00:19:18,335 --> 00:19:27,637
Part c. Content-Length is 3874. So the document has 3874 bytes.

151
00:19:28,087 --> 00:19:37,303
Part d. The document starts after the blank line. Its first 5 bytes are: less-than sign, exclamation mark, d, o, c.

152
00:19:37,753 --> 00:19:43,171
The Connection field says Keep-Alive. So the server agreed to a persistent connection.

153
00:19:43,621 --> 00:19:48,720
Question bank questions 22 to 24. Pause the video and try them.

154
00:19:49,170 --> 00:19:58,898
Question 22. Both messages are lines of ASCII text. A request starts with the request line: method, URL and version.

155
00:19:59,348 --> 00:20:04,276
A response starts with the status line: version, status code and phrase.

156
00:20:04,726 --> 00:20:08,993
Then both messages have header lines, a blank line, and the body.

157
00:20:09,443 --> 00:20:13,432
Question 23. POST sends form input in the body.

158
00:20:13,882 --> 00:20:18,725
GET sends a small amount of data in the URL, after a question mark.

159
00:20:19,175 --> 00:20:21,265
HEAD asks only for the headers.

160
00:20:21,715 --> 00:20:25,641
PUT uploads a file and replaces the file at the URL.

161
00:20:26,091 --> 00:20:32,576
Question 24. A response has a status line, header lines, a blank line, and the data.

162
00:20:33,026 --> 00:20:40,045
There are no methods in a response. Methods are only in requests. A response carries a status code.

163
00:20:40,495 --> 00:20:50,095
Now the recap. One. One object takes 2 RTT plus F over T x. Draw the time bars: open, request, and file.

164
00:20:50,545 --> 00:21:04,966
Two. For question 25, a new connection between the same end points must wait for the close, one more RTT. A connection to a new server, or a parallel connection with its own port, does not.

165
00:21:05,416 --> 00:21:15,251
Three. Parallel connections share the link rate. On a slow link, they do not help one user. But they take a larger share of the link from other users.

166
00:21:15,701 --> 00:21:23,786
Four. An HTTP message has a request line or a status line, header lines, a blank line, and a body.

167
00:21:24,236 --> 00:21:40,385
Five. POST puts data in the body, GET puts it after a question mark, HEAD returns only headers, and PUT replaces a file. Know the five status codes. The next video covers DNS. That is the end of video 5 b.
